# Product C++: C++20 + traits

## Language standard

Product C++ under **`src/`**, **`core/`**, **`testing/`**, and **`algorithm/`** is **C++20** (`cc_std = "c++20"` → MSVC `/std:c++20`). That is the floor. Do not use `/std:c++latest`. **C++23** features are allowed only when they compile with this repo's **MSVC v145 / VS 18** toolset. Linux/mac may keep `-std=c++23`.

New code and touched translation units use this standard. **`third_party/`** is out of scope — do not force third-party CMake (GDAL, etc.) onto `cc_std`. Do not rewrite **`branches/`** (if present). Legacy `src/` MFC / 2010 TUs must **compile** as C++20 once the flag is on; do not drive-by rewrite them to concepts.

## Do (portable C++20)

- Use `concepts`, `requires`, `if constexpr`, `std::invoke_result_t`, `std::is_same_v`, ranges where MSVC v145 is fine, NTTP where MSVC v145 accepts them.
- Prefer **traits + concepts** over parallel class trees, copied 2D vs 3D functions, and macros.

## Don't (language)

- Don't target C++17, or pin `/std:c++17` on product targets.
- Don't require fold-expressions-in-`requires`, GCC `__integer_pack`, or other non-MSVC constructs.
- Don't treat `/std:c++23` as the Windows default **flag** (selected C++23 features are OK; `cc_std` stays `c++20`).

## Traits / TMP

One **Geometry** hierarchy; OGC `coordinateDimension` is **2 or 3** (not parallel 2D/3D type trees). Algorithms are constrained on traits (`geometry_traits<G>`, `coordinate_type`, `dimension`, `has_z`), not on a 2D vs 3D type name. Dispatch **GEOS / PROJ / TIN** backends through traits so product types stay stable.

Keep existing **`Smt_*` virtual ABI** where LoadLibrary / DLL exports require it. New algorithms must not add another virtual geometry hierarchy.

Public namespaces stay two levels (`geo`, helpers in `geo::detail`). Traits types are public API at those two levels. New-tree functions stay `snake_case`.

YAGNI: traits exist to collapse 2D/3D and backend dispatch — not to invent an in-tree Boost.Geometry.

```cpp
namespace geo {

template <typename G>
struct geometry_traits {
  using coordinate_type = typename G::coordinate_type;
  static constexpr int dimension = G::coordinate_dimension;  // 2 or 3
  static constexpr bool has_z = dimension >= 3;
};

template <typename G>
concept geometry_like = requires {
  typename geometry_traits<G>::coordinate_type;
  requires geometry_traits<G>::dimension == 2 ||
           geometry_traits<G>::dimension == 3;
};

template <typename G>
struct geos_backend_traits;  // specialize per product type; call geos_c

template <geometry_like G>
void buffer(const G& g, typename geometry_traits<G>::coordinate_type width) {
  if constexpr (geometry_traits<G>::has_z) {
    // same Geometry type; coordinateDimension == 3
  }
  geos_backend_traits<G>::buffer(g, width);
}

}  // namespace geo
```

## Don't (product)

- Don't add Qt.
- Don't vendor a second GEOS / PROJ (use the shipped `gdal_sdk` stack).
- Don't put a parallel 2D/3D type tree next to Geometry.
